<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medina del Campo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> del Campo is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its great fairs, flourishing in the 15th and 16th centuries, made it a centre of the wool trade and of European credit and exchange. The Castillo de la Mota stands outside the town, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> died there in 1504.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo.jpg" alt="Medina del Campo" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nelso Silva · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Medina_del_Campo)_Renfe_253.072_(40033194103)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> del Campo lies on the banks of the Zapardiel river, in the central part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> basin. Initially a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> settlement, it was repopulated after 1085. It was founded on the hill called La Mota in the 11th century, on the site of the castle, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> that hill was the town centre. The hill holds remains of a stronghold, a medieval village and a Celtic walled settlement of the 4th century BCE. The walls date from the 11th century and were enlarged three times as the population grew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 12th century the walled town was a major population centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile</a> and drew large trading operations. Its free fairs flourished in the 15th and 16th centuries and supported banking and the trade in wool, textiles and books. In the 16th century the fair became the most important of the great Castilian fairs, with Villalón and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> de Rioseco, and drew merchants chiefly Castilian, Portuguese, Italian and Flemish.</p><p>In 1489 the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> del Campo united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in a trade agreement meant to last 96 years, with reduced tariffs, the recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as a common enemy and the marriage of Catherine of Aragon to Prince Arthur. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros">Revolt of the Comuneros</a> the town housed the royal artillery, and a royalist attempt to seize the guns ended in the burning of the town. Its Jewish community ended with the expulsion of 1492. From the 17th to the 19th century the town declined, and it grew again after the first railway train arrived on 3 September 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Castle of La Mota, built between the 12th and 15th centuries, served as a royal prison, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> was among its prisoners. The Royal Palace, begun in the 14th century, is where Isabel la Católica, Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, made her will and died on 26 November 1504. The Hospital of Simón Ruiz and the Palacio de Dueñas date from the 16th century, built by merchant bankers who prospered by the fair, and a Museum of the Fairs records the market.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Medina del Campo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fine wool, shipped from the Cantabrian ports by the merchants of Burgos to Flanders, became Castile's leading export, and the fairs of <b>Medina del Campo</b> made it a centre of European finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
